<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-binomial-opening> {
  ex:l5-b01-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:binary-analytical-finding ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-b01-heading, ex:l5-b01-visual ;
    cd:narration """Begin with a water investigation: use selected water samples to estimate the positive-finding frequency for one target PFAS in a defined population at the stated survey time. Specify the water area, sampling units and selection design before applying the result beyond the selected samples. We start directly with the chemistry story, not a statistics recap. Each selected sample gives one positive or negative screening classification. This is a Bernoulli trial, with positive coded 1 and negative 0; success is a statistical label, not an environmental judgement. A predefined scientific screening level is not a legal safety limit. The codes describe findings, not the actual concentration or true contamination prevalence.

A positive or negative result is a classification produced by an analytical procedure. It is not the concentration or an infallible statement about the true sample state. Negative does not prove absence. The codes 1 and 0 indicate the event being counted, not good and bad results. Two different concentrations can produce the same code. No probability calculation is required on this slide; the constant 20% teaching assumption is introduced with the repeated series next."""@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-b02-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:bernoulli-trial ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-b02-heading, ex:l5-b02-visual ;
    cd:narration """One investigation selects ten water samples from the defined population and records one screening decision per sample. Their positive count and observed positive fraction inform the unknown population finding frequency, conditional on the selection and assay process. They are not technical repetitions of one sample. We specify a common 20% probability only to simulate how surveys fluctuate; in a real investigation it is unknown. It is a teaching assumption, not an established contamination prevalence. The binomial model is an idealized independent common-probability sampling model, not proof of a representative field design.

Each of the three substantial views shows a complete simulated series together with its count and the accumulated chart. The initial seed 20261030 produces counts 1, 3 and 0. Advance only to move to the next series. These are draws from one reproducible stream, not a fixed allocation of positives. One whole series contributes one chart entry. Backward navigation restores the exact earlier series and history without collecting anything twice.

The expected count of two is an average across repeated series, not a guaranteed result. Independence means that previous negative results do not make a positive result due. The bars are collected simulated frequencies, not exact binomial probabilities. Three repetitions illustrate variability but cannot establish its distribution empirically. The next slides calculate the probability of an ordered sequence and then of a count."""@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-b01-heading a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-b01-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-b01-visual a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-b01-flow ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DiagramRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-b02-heading a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-b02-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-b02-visual a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-b02-flow ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DiagramRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
}
